# Engagement Plan, Otono Stack Reconnaissance
## Descent order I will begin by analyzing public DNS records and easily accessible HTTP headers (Layer 1) to establish an initial baseline. From there, I will progressively move deeper by analyzing reverse proxy routing behavior (Layer 2), provoking application errors (Layer 3), and deducing legacy artifacts (Layers 4 and 5), ensuring evidence gathered from the surface contextualizes the anomalies discovered below without treating initial headers as final proof.
## Request rate To honor the Rules of Engagement and minimize operational noise, I will cap concurrency at 5 parallel connections and enforce a strict 2-second delay between requests. I will also implement strict timeouts and exponential backoff for retries to guarantee zero service disruption.
## Honesty hypothesis I hypothesize that Otono will be honest at the network edge (DNS, TLS certificates) and with static asset delivery, as these require standard configurations to operate reliably. However, it will likely hide or spoof backend technologies via modified Server headers, suppressed version banners, and the two-year-old reverse proxy layer.
## Pivot signal If I observe conflicting version fingerprints—such as a modern Server header contradicting an older session cookie format or legacy backend error page—I will pause standard surface enumeration, discard the header assumption, and reprioritize targeted behavioral probes to map the true underlying application layer.
## Henrik's lesson Applied: I will treat all Server headers and declared version banners as hypotheses requiring independent behavioral verification, rather than recording them as facts. Not applied: I will not rely on cross-checking findings against internally documented stacks. Because Otono's own engineering team lacks accurate knowledge of the legacy layers, their internal documentation is unreliable for this specific engagement.
